/* ===== A4.1.2 hardware, A4.2.1 cleaning, A4.2.2 feature selection, A4.2.3 dimensionality ===== */
T.push({
  id:'a412',code:'A4.1.2',group:'Foundations',short:'Machine learning hardware',title:'Hardware requirements for machine learning',
  blurb:'CPU, GPU, TPU, ASIC, FPGA, edge and cloud.',
  verb:'Describe',cmd:'Name the hardware, explain what matters (processing, storage, scalability) and connect it to the scenario.',
  ls:'Describe the hardware requirements for various scenarios where machine learning is deployed.',
  lead:'The right hardware depends on the task. A decision tree on a few thousand rows runs on a laptop. A large language model needs thousands of specialised processors for weeks. Match hardware to a scenario by weighing <b>processing power, storage and scalability</b>, and first decide whether the scenario is <b>training or inference</b>.',
  hook:'A face-recognition model is trained once, then runs on millions of doorbells for years. Which job needs the more powerful hardware, and which one has to cope with a tiny battery?',
  stageTitle:'3D lab: matching hardware to the job',stageSub:'Training vs inference, serial vs parallel cores, and the full hardware spectrum',
  lab:{how:['On <b>Training vs inference</b>, drag the two sliders and watch the towers for processing, storage and scalability change.','On <b>CPU vs GPU</b>, press <b>Run job</b>. The CPU works through tasks one after another. The GPU does thousands at once.','On <b>Hardware spectrum</b>, press each scenario button to see which hardware wins and why. Click a tower to read about that hardware.'],
    see:['Training is heavy on processing, storage and scalability. Inference is lighter but may need low latency or very low power.','A GPU wins on matrix-style work because it has thousands of simple cores, not because each core is faster.','ASIC is fastest for one fixed job but cannot change. FPGA trades some speed for being reprogrammable.']},
  sections:[
    {tag:'Phases',h:'Training and inference: two different hardware problems',p:'Every model goes through two phases with very different needs.',ul:['<b>Training:</b> feed large amounts of data through the model and adjust its parameters to minimise error. Billions of operations per second for hours, days or weeks. <b>Processing:</b> very high, massively parallel. <b>Storage:</b> very high; whole datasets must be reachable quickly and checkpoints saved. <b>Scalability:</b> critical; training time falls when work is spread across many processors.','<b>Inference:</b> use the trained model to predict on new data. One forward pass, no backpropagation. <b>Processing:</b> moderate. <b>Storage:</b> low to moderate; only the trained parameters are kept. <b>Scalability:</b> variable; it may need to serve millions of requests or run on one low-power device. It may also need <b>low latency</b>.']},
    {tag:'Factors',h:'The three factors',ul:['<b>Processing:</b> how many operations per second, and whether they can run in parallel.','<b>Storage:</b> capacity for training data, model parameters and intermediate results, and the speed at which data can be read into the processor.','<b>Scalability:</b> ability to add more hardware to cut training time or serve more requests, from several GPUs in one machine to thousands of machines.']},
    {tag:'CPU',h:'Central Processing Unit',p:'The general-purpose processor in any laptop. Typically 4 to 32 powerful cores that run complex, varied instructions <b>sequentially</b> at high speed.',ul:['<b>Processing:</b> good for complex logic and branching; poor for the repetitive matrix multiplications that dominate ML training.','<b>Storage:</b> system RAM (fast, limited) and disk (slow). Large datasets may not fit in RAM.','<b>Scalability:</b> limited; more CPUs give marginal gains because of communication overhead.','<b>Use:</b> training only for small datasets and simple models; fine for inference where latency is not critical.','<b>Scenario:</b> a student runs logistic regression on 5,000 rows; a small business runs overnight batch inference on a simple model.']},
    {tag:'GPU',h:'Graphics Processing Unit',p:'Built for rendering graphics, a GPU holds <b>thousands of simpler cores</b> working in parallel. An NVIDIA A100 has 6,912 CUDA cores. This matches the matrix multiplications in neural networks.',ul:['<b>Processing:</b> extremely high throughput; hundreds of times faster than a CPU for neural-network training.','<b>Storage:</b> separate video memory (VRAM), typically 16 GB to 80 GB per card. Model parameters plus a batch of data must fit at once. High memory bandwidth feeds the cores.','<b>Scalability:</b> good; several GPUs per machine or across a cluster. GPU clusters are the backbone of cloud ML.','<b>Scenario:</b> a team trains a CNN on 1 million images using four GPUs; a company serves thousands of recommendation requests per second.']},
    {tag:'TPU',h:'Tensor Processing Unit',p:'A custom chip designed by Google to accelerate tensor operations in neural networks. A TPU is a type of <b>ASIC</b>: every transistor exists to speed up ML computation.',ul:['<b>Processing:</b> faster and more power-efficient than a GPU for deep-learning tensor operations; the advantage is largest with TensorFlow.','<b>Storage:</b> high-bandwidth memory built into the chip design.','<b>Scalability:</b> excellent; TPU Pods connect thousands of chips with dedicated high-speed links.','<b>Scenario:</b> Google trains a new large language model; a company uses Google Cloud ML APIs for large-scale language processing.']},
    {tag:'ASIC',h:'Application-Specific Integrated Circuit',p:'A chip made to do one task permanently. Its logic is fixed at manufacture. Examples: TPUs, Bitcoin-mining chips, face-recognition chips in smart doorbells.',ul:['<b>Processing:</b> highest possible for its task, with no wasted general-purpose logic, and useless for anything else.','<b>Storage:</b> fixed at design time. <b>Scalability:</b> limited; usually deployed as single units.','<b>Flexibility:</b> none. If the algorithm changes, a new chip must be designed and made.','<b>Cost:</b> very high upfront (millions), very low per unit at mass-production scale.','<b>Scenario:</b> a maker of 5 million smart doorbells, each doing face recognition locally with no internet.']},
    {tag:'FPGA',h:'Field-Programmable Gate Array',p:'A chip of configurable logic blocks that the user can <b>reconfigure after manufacture</b> by loading a new configuration file.',ul:['<b>Processing:</b> high for the configured function. Lower than an ASIC for the same task, much higher than a GPU where ultra-low latency matters.','<b>Storage:</b> configurable on-chip memory of moderate size. <b>Scalability:</b> moderate; networked FPGA systems are complex.','<b>Flexibility:</b> very high; this is the defining feature.','<b>Cost:</b> moderate development cost, no new fabrication when the design changes, but dearer per unit than an ASIC at scale.','<b>Scenario:</b> a trading firm prototypes a microsecond-latency ML pipeline whose algorithm is still changing.']},
    {tag:'Edge',h:'Edge devices',p:'Small, low-power units that run <b>inference</b> where the data is produced, without sending it to a server. They may use a dedicated inference chip (an ASIC), a mobile GPU or a small FPGA.',ul:['<b>Processing:</b> low to moderate, tuned for efficiency; enough for a pre-trained model, not for training.','<b>Storage:</b> severely limited; only model parameters and a small data buffer. <b>Scalability:</b> none alone, but many edge devices together process large volumes.','<b>Advantages:</b> works without a network; low latency; privacy because sensitive data never leaves the device.','<b>Scenario:</b> smart doorbell recognising faces; a tablet in a remote clinic running a diagnostic model; an autonomous vehicle processing sensor data in real time.']},
    {tag:'Cloud',h:'Cloud platforms and HPC centres',p:'Cloud platforms (AWS, Google Cloud, Azure) rent large clusters of GPUs and TPUs over the internet. HPC centres are large facilities with supercomputers, often at universities or research institutes.',ul:['<b>Processing:</b> effectively unlimited; limited by budget, not by owned hardware.','<b>Storage:</b> petabyte-scale distributed storage, separate from compute.','<b>Scalability:</b> excellent; scale up for a big run and back down afterwards, so you do not own idle hardware.','<b>Cost:</b> pay-as-you-go with no capital expense on the cloud. HPC centres usually want a research justification and a queue wait instead of payment.','<b>Scenario:</b> a start-up trains a language model on 128 rented GPUs for 48 hours; a university climate group needs more compute than its local servers give.']}
  ],
  keys:[{t:'Exam technique',html:'<b>Step 1:</b> is it training or inference? <b>Step 2:</b> find the constraints: "without internet" points to edge; "millions of units" to ASIC; "limited budget" to cloud; "prototype" or "changing design" to FPGA; "massive scale" or "large model" to a GPU cluster or TPU. <b>Step 3:</b> name the hardware, link it to processing, then comment on storage and scalability, and tie it to the scenario.'}],
  tables:[{h:'Hardware comparison',head:['Hardware','Processing','Storage','Scalability'],rows:[
    ['CPU (laptop)','Low: few powerful cores, sequential','Limited by RAM and local disk','Poor: single machine'],
    ['GPU','Very high: thousands of parallel cores','VRAM is a key limit; high bandwidth','Good: multiple GPUs can be clustered'],
    ['TPU','Extremely high for tensor operations','High-bandwidth memory in the chip','Excellent: designed for large distributed training'],
    ['ASIC','Highest for its one task','Fixed at design time','Limited: single units, fixed throughput'],
    ['FPGA','High, below an ASIC for the same task','Configurable on-chip, moderate','Moderate: can be networked but complex'],
    ['Edge device','Low to moderate, efficiency first','Minimal, limited by size and power','None alone; many devices together'],
    ['Cloud / HPC','Effectively unlimited','Petabyte-scale object storage','Excellent: scale up or down on demand']]},
   {h:'Selecting hardware for a scenario',head:['Scenario','Hardware','Justification'],rows:[
    ['Student runs a decision tree on 1,000 rows','Standard laptop (CPU)','Small data, simple model, no parallel work needed.'],
    ['Team trains a CNN on 10 million labelled images','Cloud or HPC GPU cluster','Massive parallel training; a single GPU is not enough.'],
    ['Google trains a new version of its language model','TPU cluster','Purpose-built tensor hardware with huge memory bandwidth.'],
    ['Manufacturer ships face recognition on 1 million doorbells','Edge device (custom ASIC)','Inference must run locally with no network; ASIC gives efficiency and low power at scale.'],
    ['Start-up prototypes a low-latency trading model','FPGA','Design is not final so it must be reprogrammable; microsecond latency.'],
    ['Hospital runs a skin-cancer classifier on a tablet','Edge device (mobile ASIC or GPU)','Runs locally for patient privacy and poor connectivity; low power, fast response.'],
    ['Small firm trains a sentiment model but cannot afford GPU servers','Cloud platform (rented GPUs)','No upfront cost; pay only for training hours, then run inference on cheaper hardware.']]}],
  traps:['Treating a TPU as a kind of GPU, or calling every accelerator a GPU. A GPU is a general-purpose parallel processor that suits ML. A TPU is an ASIC built for one job (tensor operations) and cannot be reprogrammed.','Saying "GPUs are fast" and stopping. Say <b>why</b> (thousands of parallel cores matched to matrix multiplication) and <b>for what</b> (training and high-throughput inference).','Not reading whether the scenario is training or inference. That decision drives every other choice.','Putting training on an edge device. Edge hardware is for inference under power and size limits.'],
  gloss:[['Training','Teaching a model by adjusting its parameters on large data; computationally heavy.'],['Inference','Using a trained model to predict on new data.'],['Latency','Delay between input and result.'],['CPU','General-purpose processor with a few powerful sequential cores.'],['GPU','Processor with thousands of simple parallel cores.'],['VRAM','Memory on the GPU itself.'],['TPU','Google ASIC built for tensor operations.'],['ASIC','Chip with fixed logic made for one task.'],['FPGA','Chip whose logic can be reconfigured after manufacture.'],['Edge device','Small low-power unit running inference locally.'],['Cloud platform','Rented compute and storage over the internet.'],['HPC centre','Facility housing supercomputers for large workloads.']],
  quiz:[
    {t:'one',q:'What is the primary architectural advantage of a GPU over a CPU for training deep learning models?',o:['It uses less electricity','It has thousands of cores for parallel processing','It has a higher clock speed','It is better at sequential tasks'],a:1,why:'Neural-network training is mostly matrix multiplication, which suits thousands of cores working in parallel.'},
    {t:'one',q:'Using a fully trained model to make a prediction on new data is called:',o:['Clustering','Inference','Training','Compiling'],a:1,why:'Training builds the model. Inference uses it.'},
    {t:'one',q:'A company makes a million smart doorbells that must recognise faces instantly on the device. Which hardware suits best?',o:['An edge device (such as a specialised ASIC)','A standard CPU','An HPC centre','A cloud platform with GPUs'],a:0,why:'Local inference with no network, low power and a huge production run: a custom ASIC edge device.'},
    {t:'one',q:'A university team must train a massive new language model from scratch. Most likely hardware?',o:['An FPGA','A cluster of hundreds of GPUs on a cloud platform','An edge device','A single high-end laptop'],a:1,why:'Very large training needs massive parallel compute that scales.'},
    {t:'one',q:'A chip designed for one single, permanent task to reach the highest performance is:',o:['A GPU','An ASIC','An FPGA','A CPU'],a:1,why:'ASIC logic is fixed at manufacture.'},
    {t:'one',q:'What distinguishes an FPGA from an ASIC?',o:['It is only used for training','It can be reconfigured after manufacturing','It is faster than an ASIC','It uses less power than an ASIC'],a:1,why:'Reprogrammability is the defining feature of an FPGA.'},
    {t:'one',q:'Google\'s TPU is a specialised type of which hardware category?',o:['GPU','ASIC','CPU','FPGA'],a:1,why:'A TPU is an ASIC built for tensor operations.'},
    {t:'one',q:'Which factor is LEAST important for an edge device doing inference?',o:['Small physical size','Low power consumption','Ability to train new models from scratch','Fast response time'],a:2,why:'Edge devices run pre-trained models. Training happens elsewhere.'},
    {t:'one',q:'Why might a start-up with a limited budget rent a cloud platform rather than build its own HPC centre?',o:['Cloud is always faster','HPC cannot be used for ML','It avoids a massive upfront cost and gives scalability','Only the cloud has GPUs'],a:2,why:'Pay-as-you-go removes capital expense and scales on demand.'},
    {t:'one',q:'Training a model is typically associated with which environment?',o:['Edge devices','Standard laptops','FPGAs','Cloud platforms or HPC centres'],a:3,why:'Training is heavy on processing, storage and scalability.'},
    {t:'multi',q:'Select <b>all</b> true statements about edge devices.',o:['They run inference near where data is produced','They keep sensitive data on the device','They need a constant internet connection','They are normally used to train large models'],a:[0,1],why:'Edge devices give local, private, low-latency inference without a network.'},
    {t:'tf',q:'A TPU can be reprogrammed to do any task, like an FPGA.',a:false,why:'A TPU is an ASIC with fixed logic. An FPGA is the reconfigurable one.'}
  ],
  paper:[
    {q:'Describe the difference in hardware requirements between training a machine learning model and using it for inference.',m:4,pts:['Training needs very high processing power, doing billions of operations, usually massively parallel.','Training needs very high storage so whole datasets and checkpoints can be read quickly.','Inference needs only moderate processing: a single forward pass with no backpropagation.','Inference needs little storage (only the trained parameters) but may need low latency or very low power.']},
    {q:'A manufacturer plans one million smart doorbells that recognise faces on the device itself with no internet connection. Describe the most suitable hardware and justify your choice.',m:4,pts:['An <b>edge device using a custom ASIC</b>.','Inference runs locally so no network is needed.','The ASIC gives the highest efficiency and low power for one fixed task.','The high upfront design cost is recovered across a million units.']},
    {q:'A start-up is still changing the design of a low-latency trading system and needs hardware it can alter as the architecture evolves. Describe why an FPGA suits this better than an ASIC.',m:3,pts:['An FPGA can be <b>reconfigured</b> after manufacture by loading a new configuration.','An ASIC is fixed, so any design change needs a new chip to be designed and made.','FPGAs still give very low (microsecond) latency and have moderate development cost.']}
  ]
});

T.push({
  id:'a421',code:'A4.2.1',group:'Preparing data',short:'Data cleaning',title:'The significance of data cleaning',
  blurb:'GIGO, six data problems, scaling.',
  verb:'Describe',cmd:'Name the technique, explain the problem it solves, describe how it works, and say why it matters for model performance.',
  ls:'Describe the significance of data cleaning.',
  lead:'Raw data is almost never ready for a model. <b>Data cleaning</b> detects and corrects problems before training. A model cannot question a strange value, so <b>Garbage In, Garbage Out (GIGO)</b>: poor data gives a poor model, however clever the design. Data scientists often spend <b>60 to 80%</b> of a project on cleaning and preprocessing.',
  hook:'A loan model has two features: annual income (15,000 to 250,000) and credit score (300 to 850). Which one might a distance-based algorithm wrongly treat as more important, and why?',
  stageTitle:'3D lab: the dirty-data workshop',stageSub:'See GIGO, fix the six problems, and rescale features',
  lab:{how:['On <b>Garbage in, garbage out</b>, toggle dirty data and watch the fitted line tilt away from the true pattern.','On <b>Six problems</b>, pick a problem tab, press <b>Fix it</b> and read the technique used.','On <b>Scaling</b>, switch between raw, min-max and z-score and watch the salary and age axes become comparable.'],
    see:['One extreme outlier can drag a regression line away from where most data lies.','Duplicates make a record count more than once. Inconsistent formats split one category into several.','After scaling, no feature wins just because its numbers are bigger.']},
  sections:[
    {tag:'GIGO',h:'Why data quality decides model quality',p:'A model has no outside reference. It learns whatever patterns are in the data and reproduces them. Poor data causes three problems:',ol:['<b>Factual errors</b> (such as wrongly labelled medical images) teach false relationships that carry into predictions.','<b>Historical bias</b> (such as a hiring set under-representing some groups) is learned and often amplified, giving discriminatory decisions.','<b>Noise and irrelevant features</b> let the model fit patterns that exist only in the training set, so it generalises poorly.']},
    {tag:'1',h:'Missing data',p:'One or more values are absent: a blank age field, a sensor that failed to log. Many algorithms cannot process null values. Dropping rows can bias the data if missingness is not random.',ul:['<b>Deletion:</b> remove rows with missing values. Simple, but may lose a lot of data and introduce bias.','<b>Imputation:</b> replace with the <b>mean</b> (average), <b>median</b> (middle value, less affected by outliers) or <b>mode</b> (most frequent, for categories). Keeps every row but adds artificial values.','<b>Predictive modelling:</b> train a model to predict the missing value from the other features (for example salary from job title, experience, location). More accurate, but costs more and adds complexity.']},
    {tag:'2',h:'Outliers',p:'A value statistically far from the rest: age 210; a house price of 5 where prices run from 100,000 to 2,000,000. An outlier is <b>not automatically an error</b>: a billionaire in an income dataset is genuine. One extreme value can drag a regression line away from most of the data.',ul:['<b>Investigate first:</b> check the source, use domain knowledge or ask an expert.','<b>Remove</b> confirmed errors with no correct replacement (document the decision if removing a genuine extreme).','<b>Cap (Winsorisation):</b> replace values beyond a threshold with the threshold, for example cap every age above 100 at 100.','Also worth knowing: a <b>log transformation</b> compresses a heavily skewed feature spanning several orders of magnitude.']},
    {tag:'3',h:'Incorrect data',p:'Values that are factually wrong, not just unusual: date of birth 2045, salary of -50,000, country code "XZ", a mislabelled record. The model learns false relationships and the damage is invisible in training.',ul:['<b>Validation rules:</b> age between 0 and 120, salary positive, country code in a reference list. Flag or reject rule-breakers.','<b>Cross-checking:</b> compare to a related field or external reference (a postcode that does not match the stated city).','<b>Correction or removal:</b> correct if the true value can be found; otherwise treat the field as missing.']},
    {tag:'4',h:'Duplicate data',p:'The same record appears more than once: exact duplicates, or near-duplicates such as "J. Smith" and "John Smith" at one address. The model sees them more often than their true frequency, weights them too much and can overfit to them.',ul:['<b>Remove or consolidate:</b> keep one of each set of identical rows; use matching algorithms on names and addresses to merge near-duplicates into one canonical record.']},
    {tag:'5',h:'Irrelevant data',p:'Features with no logical link to the target: a telephone number when predicting purchases, a unique ID when predicting house prices. They add noise, invite spurious correlations (worst when features outnumber examples), lengthen training and use memory.',ul:['<b>Feature selection:</b> drop near-zero-variance features, logically unrelated features, and features that a statistical test shows are unrelated to the target.']},
    {tag:'6',h:'Improperly formatted data',p:'One real value written many ways: "USA", "U.S.A.", "United States", "america"; dates mixing DD/MM/YYYY and MM/DD/YYYY; inconsistent capitalisation. The model treats each string as a different category and splits a pattern into weak fragments.',ul:['<b>Format standardisation:</b> convert country names to ISO codes, dates to ISO 8601 (YYYY-MM-DD) and apply consistent capitalisation, before later steps depend on them.']},
    {tag:'Scaling',h:'Normalisation and standardisation',p:'After cleaning, numeric features can sit on very different scales (salary 20,000 to 500,000 against age 18 to 90). Distance-based algorithms may then treat salary as more important just because the numbers are bigger. Rescaling makes features comparable.',ul:['<b>Normalisation (min-max):</b> rescales to a fixed range, usually 0 to 1. <code>x\' = (x - x_min) / (x_max - x_min)</code>. Best when the range is known and bounded and outliers are not a concern.','<b>Standardisation (z-score):</b> rescales to mean 0 and standard deviation 1. <code>x\' = (x - mean) / standard deviation</code>. Best when there is no fixed range or outliers are present, since it is less affected by them.','<b>Standard deviation</b> measures spread: low means values cluster near the mean, high means they are spread widely.']},
    {tag:'Example',h:'Worked example: cleaning one record',eg:true,ul:['<b>Name</b> "alice smith": improperly formatted (capitalisation). After: "Alice Smith".','<b>Age</b> 210: outlier and incorrect data. Investigate, confirm an error, remove the row (or correct to 21 if verifiable).','<b>Salary</b> 55000: plausible, no change.','<b>Country</b> "U.S.A.": improperly formatted. After: "USA".','<b>Purchase?</b> (empty): missing. Imputed with the column mean 0.52.','<b>Customer_ID</b> "C-00142": irrelevant, a unique ID has no predictive value. Column removed.']},
    {tag:'Maths',h:'Scaling worked values',eg:true,ul:['Min-max: salary 55,000 with min 20,000 and max 200,000 gives (55,000 - 20,000) / (200,000 - 20,000) = <b>0.194</b>.','Z-score: salary 55,000 with mean 80,000 and standard deviation 30,000 gives (55,000 - 80,000) / 30,000 = <b>-0.83</b>, which is 0.83 standard deviations below the mean.']}
  ],
  tables:[{h:'Outliers versus incorrect data',head:['','Outlier','Incorrect data'],rows:[
    ['Judged against','The distribution of the rest of the data','The real world'],
    ['Can it be genuine?','Yes (a billionaire\'s salary)','No, it is factually wrong (negative salary)'],
    ['How detected','Statistical distance from the bulk','Validation rules and cross-checking'],
    ['First response','Investigate whether real or an error','Correct it, or treat the field as missing']]},
   {h:'Summary reference',head:['Problem','Description','If ignored','Technique'],rows:[
    ['Missing data','Empty cells where a value should exist','Errors on nulls; bias if missingness is not random','Deletion; mean, median or mode imputation; predictive modelling'],
    ['Outliers','Values far from the rest','Model over-weights extremes, skews predictions','Investigate, then remove, cap (Winsorise) or log transform'],
    ['Incorrect data','Factually wrong values','Model learns false patterns','Validation rules; cross-checking; correction or removal'],
    ['Duplicate data','Identical or near-identical records','Over-weighting and overfitting','Remove exact duplicates; consolidate near-duplicates'],
    ['Irrelevant data','Features with no link to the target','Noise, spurious correlations, slower training','Feature selection'],
    ['Improperly formatted','Same value written several ways','Categories fragment, patterns dilute','Format standardisation']]}],
  keys:[{t:'Key idea',html:'A model can only be as good as the data it learns from. No amount of tuning repairs patterns that are wrong, missing or biased in the training set.'}],
  traps:['Treating outliers and incorrect data as the same. An outlier is judged against the dataset; incorrect data is judged against reality. A value can be both (age 210), so <b>investigate before acting</b>.','Assuming every outlier is an error and should be removed. A genuine extreme can be the most valuable signal.','"Standardisation" is used for two things. <b>Format standardisation</b> makes text consistent (country names). <b>Statistical standardisation</b> (z-score) rescales numbers to mean 0 and SD 1. Say which you mean.','Describing deletion as harmless. It throws away other useful values in the row and can bias the data.'],
  gloss:[['Data cleaning','Detecting and correcting problems in data before training.'],['GIGO','Garbage in, garbage out: poor input data gives a poor model.'],['Imputation','Replacing missing values with a calculated substitute.'],['Outlier','A value statistically far from the rest of the data.'],['Winsorisation','Capping extreme values at a chosen threshold.'],['Validation rule','A constraint valid values must satisfy.'],['Near-duplicate','The same entity recorded with small variations.'],['Normalisation','Min-max rescaling to a fixed range, usually 0 to 1.'],['Standardisation','Z-score rescaling to mean 0, standard deviation 1.'],['Standard deviation','A measure of how spread out values are.']],
  quiz:[
    {t:'one',q:'The principle that the quality of a model\'s output is set by the quality of its input data is:',o:['Moore\'s Law','"Garbage In, Garbage Out"','The Turing Test','The "No Free Lunch" theorem'],a:1,why:'GIGO.'},
    {t:'one',q:'A student\'s age is recorded as 210 in school records. Against the rest of the data this is best described as:',o:['Missing data','A duplicate','Irrelevant data','An outlier'],a:3,why:'It sits far from the rest of the distribution. It is also impossible, so it is incorrect data too.'},
    {t:'one',q:'A model predicting house prices includes each homeowner\'s unique ID number. This column is:',o:['Missing data','Duplicate data','An outlier','Irrelevant data'],a:3,why:'A unique ID has no logical connection to price.'},
    {t:'tf',q:'An outlier is always an error and should be removed.',a:false,why:'A billionaire\'s salary is a genuine extreme. Investigate first.'},
    {t:'one',q:'A "City" column holds "New York", "new york" and "NY". Converting them to one agreed form is:',o:['Normalisation','Imputation','Format standardisation','Deletion'],a:2,why:'Making representations consistent is format standardisation.'},
    {t:'multi',q:'Select <b>all</b> techniques used to handle <b>missing</b> data.',o:['Deletion','Mean imputation','Winsorisation','Feature selection','Format standardisation'],a:[0,1],why:'Deletion, imputation and predictive modelling handle missing data. Winsorisation is for outliers.'},
    {t:'one',q:'Which technique for missing data discards other, possibly useful, values in the same record?',o:['Imputation with the median','Predictive modelling','Deletion','Imputation with the mean'],a:2,why:'Deleting the row loses the rest of that record.'},
    {t:'one',q:'Min-max normalisation rescales a feature to which range?',o:['-1 to 1','0 to 1','Mean 0 and SD 1','0 to 100'],a:1,why:'Min-max gives a fixed range, usually 0 to 1.'},
    {t:'one',q:'Statistical standardisation (z-score) gives a feature:',o:['Range 0 to 1','Mean 0 and standard deviation 1','Median 1','All values equal'],a:1,why:'z = (x - mean) / SD.'},
    {t:'one',q:'A salary of 55,000 with mean 80,000 and SD 30,000 has a z-score of:',o:['-0.83','0.83','-25,000','0.194'],a:0,why:'(55,000 - 80,000) / 30,000 = -0.83.'},
    {t:'one',q:'Capping every age above 100 at 100 is called:',o:['Imputation','Winsorisation','Deduplication','Normalisation'],a:1,why:'Replacing values beyond a threshold with the threshold is Winsorisation.'},
    {t:'one',q:'A salary of 250,000 belongs to an intern. Outlier detection does not flag it, yet it is wrong. Which category is it?',o:['Outlier','Incorrect data','Duplicate','Missing'],a:1,why:'Wrong against the real world, not statistically extreme. Validation rules and cross-checking catch it.'}
  ],
  paper:[
    {q:'Describe two of the six categories of data problem. For each, state the problem and describe one technique used to address it.',m:4,pts:['Problem 1 stated clearly (for example: duplicate data is the same record appearing more than once).','A technique for problem 1 described (for example: remove exact duplicates and consolidate near-duplicates into one canonical record).','Problem 2 stated clearly (for example: missing data is an absent value in a record).','A technique for problem 2 described (for example: impute with the mean, median or mode).']},
    {q:'Describe normalisation and describe standardisation as methods of feature scaling.',m:4,pts:['Normalisation (min-max) rescales a feature to a fixed range, usually 0 to 1.','Formula (x - min) / (max - min); suits a known, bounded range with no outlier concern.','Standardisation (z-score) rescales to mean 0 and standard deviation 1.','Formula (x - mean) / SD; suits no fixed range or when outliers are present. Both stop large-valued features dominating distance-based algorithms.']}
  ]
});

T.push({
  id:'a422',code:'A4.2.2',group:'Preparing data',short:'Feature selection',title:'Feature selection',
  blurb:'Filter, wrapper and embedded methods.',
  verb:'Describe',cmd:'State the role of feature selection, describe how each strategy works, name a technique for each, and give its trade-off.',
  ls:'Describe the role of feature selection (filter, wrapper and embedded methods).',
  lead:'After cleaning, which columns should the model see? <b>Feature selection</b> keeps only the informative features. What separates the three strategies is <b>when</b> selection happens: <b>filter</b> before training, <b>wrapper</b> around training, <b>embedded</b> during training.',
  hook:'You must choose the 20 most useful books out of 500. You could score each book alone, try many shelves of 20 and test which helps most, or read once and note which books you kept using. Which is fastest, and which might pick five books on the same topic?',
  stageTitle:'3D lab: three ways to pick features',stageSub:'Score them, search subsets, or let the model shrink them',
  lab:{how:['On <b>Filter</b>, drag the threshold plane up and down. Features whose correlation bars poke above it are kept.','On <b>Wrapper</b>, choose Forward, Backward or RFE and press <b>Step</b>. Watch how many models get trained.','On <b>Embedded (Lasso)</b>, raise the penalty and watch coefficient bars shrink to exactly zero.'],
    see:['Filter is cheap because nothing is trained, but it scores features one at a time.','Wrapper tests real subsets so it is accurate for one model, but the cost explodes with feature count.','Lasso removes features inside a single training run, so selection costs nothing extra.']},
  sections:[
    {tag:'Role',h:'Why select features',p:'A <b>feature</b> is a column describing one attribute. A feature is <b>irrelevant</b> when it has no real relationship with the target, and <b>redundant</b> when another feature already carries the same information. Extra features harm a model in three ways:',ol:['<b>Overfitting:</b> too many features let the model fit coincidental patterns: strong on seen data, weak on new data.','<b>Computation:</b> ten features train quickly; ten thousand can take hours or days longer for no gain.','<b>Explainability:</b> five well-chosen features are easier to audit than five hundred, which matters in medicine, finance and law.'],p2:'This builds on A4.2.1, where obviously useless columns (IDs, phone numbers) are removed by inspection. Feature selection uses statistical tests or the model itself to find features that are only subtly irrelevant or redundant.'},
    {tag:'Filter',h:'Filter methods: before training',p:'Selection is a preprocessing step, independent of any learning algorithm. Each feature is scored on its own with a statistical measure of its link to the target. Features above a threshold stay.',ul:['<b>Measures:</b> Pearson <b>correlation</b> (numeric feature vs numeric target); <b>chi-squared</b> test (categorical vs categorical); <b>mutual information</b> (how much knowing the feature reduces uncertainty about the target; works for both); <b>variance threshold</b> (drop features that are nearly constant).','<b>Cost:</b> very cheap, because nothing is trained. Often the first pass over many features.','<b>Limitation:</b> scoring one at a time misses interactions (a weak feature may help in combination) and can keep two features that duplicate each other.','<b>Analogy:</b> a librarian scores 500 books by title and summary alone and may pick five on the same narrow topic.']},
    {tag:'Wrapper',h:'Wrapper methods: around training',p:'The model is part of the selection. A subset is chosen, a model is trained and evaluated, the score is recorded, and this repeats for many subsets. The best subset wins.',ul:['<b>Forward selection:</b> start with no features; add the one that improves performance most, until nothing worthwhile remains.','<b>Backward elimination:</b> start with all features; remove the one whose loss hurts least, repeatedly.','<b>Recursive feature elimination (RFE):</b> train on all features, rank by importance, remove the least important, retrain, repeat until the target number remains. The most common wrapper method.','<b>Cost:</b> expensive, because a model is retrained many times and the number of subsets grows exponentially.','<b>Limitation:</b> the chosen features are tuned to one model and may not carry to another.','<b>Analogy:</b> an optometrist tries many lens combinations; accurate for that patient, slower than measuring the eye.']},
    {tag:'Embedded',h:'Embedded methods: during training',p:'Some algorithms judge feature importance while they train. Unimportant features end up with weights near zero, and selection is a by-product of one training run.',ul:['<b>Lasso regression (L1 regularisation):</b> adds a penalty proportional to the absolute size of each coefficient, pushing unimportant coefficients to <b>exactly zero</b>. Features with non-zero coefficients are selected.','<b>Decision trees and ensembles (Random Forest):</b> each split uses the feature that most reduces impurity, so how often and how usefully a feature is used gives an importance score. Rarely used features are dropped.','<b>Cost:</b> none beyond the training you were going to do.','<b>Limitation:</b> only for algorithms that produce importance scores, and the scores belong to that algorithm.','<b>Analogy:</b> a recipe that quietly records which ingredients were used most in the best dishes.']},
    {tag:'Example',h:'Worked example: filter on house prices',eg:true,p:'Pearson correlation against sale price, threshold 0.5.',ul:['square_footage 0.87: <b>keep</b>','num_bedrooms 0.74: <b>keep</b>','age_of_property 0.61: <b>keep</b>','postcode 0.09: remove (a raw string is not numeric; in practice you would encode it, not discard it)','listing_ref 0.02: remove (identifier)','prev_owner_name 0.00: remove (identifier)','Result: three features instead of six, faster training, and no way to latch on to an owner\'s name.']}
  ],
  tables:[{h:'Comparing the three strategies',head:['Strategy','When selection occurs','Named techniques','Cost'],rows:[
    ['Filter','Before training, independent of any model','Correlation, chi-squared, mutual information, variance threshold','Low: scores each feature once'],
    ['Wrapper','Around training; the model evaluates each subset','Forward selection, backward elimination, RFE','Very high: trains a model per subset'],
    ['Embedded','During training, built into the algorithm','Lasso regression, decision tree importance','Low: a by-product of one training run']]}],
  keys:[{t:'Key idea',html:'Feature selection keeps attributes that carry predictive signal and drops those that add cost or noise without information. Fewer, better features usually beat more. The deciding difference is <b>timing</b>: filter before, wrapper around, embedded during.'}],
  traps:['Naming only the mechanism. For embedded methods say <b>Lasso regression</b>; for wrapper say <b>forward selection, backward elimination or RFE</b>. "The model is trained on subsets" alone will not reach full marks.','Saying filter methods train a model. They do not. They score features with a statistic.','Confusing feature selection with dimensionality reduction (A4.2.3). Selection keeps a subset of the original columns unchanged.'],
  gloss:[['Feature','A column of the dataset; one attribute of each example.'],['Target','The value the model predicts.'],['Irrelevant feature','No real relationship with the target.'],['Redundant feature','Duplicates information another feature gives.'],['Overfitting','Fitting noise so the model generalises poorly.'],['Filter method','Scores features with statistics before any model is trained.'],['Wrapper method','Evaluates feature subsets by training a model on each.'],['Embedded method','Feature importance emerges during a single training run.'],['RFE','Repeatedly train, rank and drop the least important feature.'],['Lasso','Regression with an L1 penalty that shrinks some coefficients to zero.']],
  quiz:[
    {t:'one',q:'The primary goal of feature selection is to:',o:['Make sure the data is clean','Make the dataset larger','Select the most informative attributes to improve model performance','Add complexity to the model'],a:2,why:'Keep informative features, drop the rest.'},
    {t:'one',q:'A model that learns irrelevant noise instead of the true pattern is:',o:['Overfitting','A wrapper method','Underfitting','Standardised'],a:0,why:'That is overfitting.'},
    {t:'tf',q:'Filter methods must train a model before they can score a feature.',a:false,why:'Filters use statistics and run before any model is trained.'},
    {t:'one',q:'___ methods select features before any model is trained; ___ methods select them as a by-product of a single training run.',o:['Wrapper, filter','Filter, embedded','Embedded, wrapper','Filter, wrapper'],a:1,why:'Filter before; embedded during.'},
    {t:'order',q:'Put the steps of recursive feature elimination (RFE) in order.',o:['Train the model on all features','Rank the features by importance','Remove the least important feature','Retrain the model','Repeat until the target number of features remains'],why:'Train, rank, remove, retrain, repeat.'},
    {t:'multi',q:'Select <b>all</b> true statements about wrapper methods.',o:['They can capture interactions between features','They are the cheapest strategy','The features they pick are tuned to the specific model used','They score each feature independently of any model','They never need a model trained'],a:[0,2],why:'Wrappers train real models on subsets, so they see interactions but are expensive and model-specific.'},
    {t:'one',q:'Which strategy evaluates subsets of features by training a model on each, by trial and error?',o:['Embedded methods','Imputation','Wrapper methods','Filter methods'],a:2,why:'That is the wrapper approach.'},
    {t:'one',q:'In a house-price model, which feature is the best candidate for removal?',o:['Number of bedrooms','Square footage','Age of the house','The previous owner\'s name'],a:3,why:'An owner\'s name has no bearing on price.'},
    {t:'tf',q:'Lasso regression performs feature selection during training by shrinking unimportant coefficients to exactly zero.',a:true,why:'The L1 penalty drives unimportant coefficients to zero, so those features are dropped.'},
    {t:'one',q:'Which is a weakness of filter methods?',o:['They are very expensive','They cannot see feature interactions','They need a model trained for every subset','They only work with decision trees'],a:1,why:'Scoring features one at a time misses combinations and duplicated information.'}
  ],
  paper:[
    {q:'Describe the three feature selection strategies. For each, state when selection occurs relative to model training and name one technique it uses.',m:4,pts:['Filter: selects <b>before</b> training; example technique correlation, chi-squared, mutual information or variance threshold.','Wrapper: selects <b>around</b> training by evaluating subsets with the model; example forward selection, backward elimination or RFE.','Embedded: selects <b>during</b> training as a by-product; example Lasso regression or decision tree importance.','All three timings correct with a technique each (one overall mark for completeness).']},
    {q:'Describe how wrapper methods differ from filter methods in the way they evaluate features.',m:2,pts:['Filter methods score each feature independently with a statistical measure, without training a model.','Wrapper methods train and evaluate a model on candidate subsets of features and keep the best-performing subset.']}
  ]
});

T.push({
  id:'a423',code:'A4.2.3',group:'Preparing data',short:'Dimensionality reduction',title:'The importance of dimensionality reduction',
  blurb:'The curse of dimensionality, seven considerations.',
  verb:'Describe',cmd:'For each consideration state the problem and give a detailed account of its effect on the model or the analysis.',
  ls:'Describe the importance of dimensionality reduction.',
  lead:'<b>Dimensionality</b> is the number of features. More features sound like more information, but past a point they damage accuracy, slow training and make data hard to analyse. These problems are the <b>curse of dimensionality</b>, and <b>dimensionality reduction</b> is the response. (PCA and LDA are named in the syllabus but their mathematics is out of scope.)',
  hook:'100 data points sit close together on a line, spread out on a square, and further apart in a cube. What happens as you keep adding dimensions? When do 100 points tell you almost nothing about the space they live in?',
  stageTitle:'3D lab: the curse of dimensionality',stageSub:'Watch space empty out, distances collapse and features merge',
  lab:{how:['On <b>Sparsity</b>, press 1D, 2D and 3D. The same 27 points spread into more and more empty space.','On <b>Distances</b>, drag the dimension slider from 1 up to 200 and watch the nearest and furthest distance towers meet.','On <b>Seven considerations</b>, click each glowing orb to read what happens and its impact.','On <b>Selection vs reduction</b>, press each button to see columns dropped versus columns merged.'],
    see:['The volume grows far faster than the points can fill it.','At high dimensions the ratio of nearest to furthest distance approaches 1, so "nearest" means nothing.','Selection keeps original columns. Reduction builds new composite columns and the originals no longer exist.']},
  sections:[
    {tag:'Curse',h:'What the curse of dimensionality is',p:'A dimension is one feature. A house dataset might have five; a medical dataset five hundred. The curse is the set of problems that appear in high-dimensional data. They come from how space behaves as dimensions grow, not from a bug, and no careful coding removes them.'},
    {tag:'1',h:'Data sparsity',p:'With a fixed number of points, more dimensions spread them further apart and most of the space holds no data. Algorithms that look for local neighbourhoods have nothing to work with.',ul:['<b>Example:</b> 1,000 patient records are dense in 5 dimensions. Give each patient 1,000 dimensions (one per test) and the same patients become isolated points in a space so large no two are meaningfully close.']},
    {tag:'2',h:'Overfitting',p:'Many features give many parameters (about one weight each plus interactions). A model with more flexibility than the problem needs memorises coincidences. High training accuracy, poor performance on new data.',ul:['<b>Example:</b> a spam filter with 10,000 word features learns that one sender address means spam and fires on that alone. With 50 chosen features this is far less likely.']},
    {tag:'3',h:'Computational complexity',p:'Every feature adds calculations to each training step, and training repeats many times. A model that trains in seconds on 10 features may take hours on 1,000 and days on 10,000.',ul:['<b>Example:</b> a 512 by 512 colour image has over 786,000 input values. Training on thousands of them without reducing the representation is prohibitively expensive.']},
    {tag:'4',h:'Effectiveness of distance metrics',p:'k-NN, k-means and SVMs use distance as similarity. In high dimensions the nearest and furthest distances become almost equal, so every point looks about equally far from every other. "Nearest" loses meaning and predictions become unreliable.',ul:['<b>Example:</b> k-NN picks the 3 most similar patients in 5 dimensions. In 500 dimensions the distances to all patients converge, so there is no basis for choosing which 3 to trust.','<b>Worked case:</b> with every feature in 0 to 1, by 100 dimensions the distance between random point pairs converges near 1.41, and the nearest/furthest ratio tends to 1. This is a property of high-dimensional geometry. The only fix is fewer dimensions.']},
    {tag:'5',h:'Data visualisation',p:'People can read two or three dimensions. Beyond that there is no direct way to draw the data, so exploring clusters, outliers and relationships is blocked until the data is reduced to 2D or 3D.',ul:['<b>Example:</b> 1,000 customers with 200 features cannot be plotted. Reduced to 2 dimensions, three distinct groups may be visible at once.']},
    {tag:'6',h:'Sample size increases',p:'To keep adequate density, the examples needed grow <b>exponentially</b> with dimensions. Real datasets cannot grow to match, so high-dimensional models run on far less data than their space needs.',ul:['<b>Example:</b> a fraud model on 10 features may learn from 10,000 transactions. With 100 features it could need 10 million to hold the same density.']},
    {tag:'7',h:'Memory usage',p:'Memory for a dataset scales with examples times features, and operations like matrix multiplication need working memory that grows too. If data exceeds RAM the system falls back to slow disk.',ul:['<b>Example:</b> 500,000 patients with 1 million genetic variants is 500 billion values, roughly 4 terabytes, beyond most machines.']},
    {tag:'Fix',h:'What dimensionality reduction does',p:'It reduces the number of features while <b>preserving</b> as much important information and structure as possible. It counters all seven considerations at once: denser data, less overfitting, faster training, meaningful distances, visualisation becomes possible, lower sample-size demand, less memory.',ul:['It <b>transforms</b> the original features into a smaller set of <b>composite features</b>, each a mathematical combination of several originals.','<b>Example:</b> height, weight, arm span and leg length are highly correlated. They can be combined into one composite feature for overall body size.','Methods such as PCA and LDA do this but are beyond the course.']}
  ],
  tables:[{h:'Feature selection vs dimensionality reduction',head:['Dimension','Feature selection','Dimensionality reduction'],rows:[
    ['What it does','Selects a subset of the original features and discards the rest','Transforms the originals into a smaller set of composite features'],
    ['Original features kept?','Yes, unchanged','No, they are combined into a new representation'],
    ['Interpretability','High: kept features keep their meaning (age, income)','Lower: composites may have no intuitive meaning'],
    ['When to use','Some features are clearly irrelevant or redundant','Most features carry some information but there are too many'],
    ['Example','Keep square_footage and num_bedrooms; drop listing_ref','Combine height and weight into one body-size feature']]},
   {h:'The seven considerations',head:['Consideration','What happens','Impact'],rows:[
    ['Data sparsity','Points spread out; empty regions','No meaningful local neighbourhoods'],
    ['Overfitting','Excess flexibility from many features','High training accuracy, poor on new data'],
    ['Computational complexity','Each feature adds calculations','Training time rises sharply'],
    ['Effectiveness of distance metrics','Nearest and furthest distances converge','k-NN and k-means cannot tell near from far'],
    ['Data visualisation','Cannot plot beyond 3 dimensions','Exploration and debugging harder'],
    ['Sample size increases','Examples needed grow exponentially','Datasets cannot keep up'],
    ['Memory usage','More memory per example and for operations','May exceed RAM; slower disk or limited models']]}],
  traps:['Describing dimensionality reduction as "choosing which features to keep". That is feature selection. Reduction <b>creates new composite features</b> and the originals no longer exist.','Naming a consideration without detail. A describe answer needs what the problem is <b>and</b> its effect on the model or the analysis.','Thinking more features always means a better model. Beyond a point, more features hurt.'],
  gloss:[['Dimension','One feature; one column of the dataset.'],['Curse of dimensionality','Problems that arise in high-dimensional data.'],['Data sparsity','Points spread thinly through a mostly empty space.'],['Composite feature','A new feature built as a combination of several originals.'],['Dimensionality reduction','Fewer features while preserving important structure.'],['PCA / LDA','Statistical methods for reduction (out of scope).']],
  quiz:[
    {t:'one',q:'The "curse of dimensionality" refers to problems that come from having:',o:['Too few data points','Data that is not normalised','Too many features (columns)','Too many duplicate records'],a:2,why:'It is about high-dimensional data.'},
    {t:'one',q:'As dimensions increase while the number of points stays the same, the data becomes more:',o:['Biased','Sparse','Linear','Dense'],a:1,why:'The points spread out through a much larger space.'},
    {t:'one',q:'A model that is perfect on training data but fails on new data is likely:',o:['Underfitting','Low in computational complexity','Overfitting','Suffering data sparsity only'],a:2,why:'That is the signature of overfitting.'},
    {t:'one',q:'Distance becomes a poor similarity measure in high dimensions because:',o:['The distance formula changes','Nearest and furthest distances converge so "nearest" loses meaning','The data becomes easier to visualise','All points collapse to one point'],a:1,why:'All points end up roughly equidistant.'},
    {t:'multi',q:'Select <b>all</b> considerations of the curse of dimensionality.',o:['Overfitting','Underfitting','Increased memory usage','Data becoming easier to plot','Faster training'],a:[0,2],why:'Overfitting and memory use grow with dimensions. Plotting gets harder, training slower.'},
    {t:'tf',q:'Dimensionality reduction works by selecting the most important original features and discarding the rest.',a:false,why:'That describes feature selection. Reduction builds new composite features.'},
    {t:'one',q:'A data scientist turns 200 features into 10 new features, each a combination of the originals. This is:',o:['Dimensionality reduction','Standardisation','Data cleaning','Feature selection'],a:0,why:'New composites replace the originals.'},
    {t:'one',q:'Which consideration means a medical study cannot simply collect 100 times more patients when it adds 100 more variables?',o:['Sample size increases','Memory usage','Data visualisation','Overfitting'],a:0,why:'Examples needed grow exponentially with dimensions.'},
    {t:'one',q:'Which consideration is that 1,000 customers with 200 features cannot be drawn directly?',o:['Data visualisation','Data sparsity','Memory usage','Distance metrics'],a:0,why:'People can only read two or three dimensions.'},
    {t:'one',q:'Which is a benefit of feature selection over dimensionality reduction?',o:['Kept features keep their original meaning','It always removes more features','It creates composite features','It needs no data'],a:0,why:'Selection keeps original columns, so interpretability stays high.'}
  ],
  paper:[
    {q:'Describe two considerations of the curse of dimensionality, including the effect of each on a model.',m:4,pts:['Consideration 1 named and explained (for example data sparsity: points spread apart as dimensions rise leaving most of the space empty).','Effect on the model for 1 (algorithms relying on local neighbourhoods find none).','Consideration 2 named and explained (for example overfitting: many features give excess flexibility).','Effect for 2 (high training accuracy but poor performance on new data).']},
    {q:'Describe how dimensionality reduction differs from feature selection.',m:4,pts:['Feature selection keeps a <b>subset of the original</b> features and discards the rest.','Dimensionality reduction <b>transforms</b> the originals into a smaller set of new composite features.','In selection the kept features are unchanged and interpretable; in reduction the originals no longer exist and composites may have no intuitive meaning.','Reduction suits cases where most features carry some information but there are too many; selection suits clearly irrelevant or redundant features. Example, such as height and weight combined into body size.']}
  ]
});
